1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,475
Block three.

2
00:00:01,475 --> 00:00:03,800
When code does not work.

3
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:10,550
On the page, this block is the sections "When you don't know how to do something", "Remember" and "How to use chatbots".

4
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:14,250
Everything so far has been about getting things right.

5
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:22,125
This block is about what to do when you have not, which is most of the time, and it is the most practically useful half-hour in the course.

6
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:29,325
A routine, five steps, and then three specific traps: the documentation, the AI assistant, and the notebook.

7
00:00:29,925 --> 00:00:34,025
Experienced programmers are not people who make fewer mistakes.

8
00:00:34,025 --> 00:00:40,425
They are people who have a routine for getting out of them, so that being stuck is a procedure rather than a mood.

9
00:00:40,425 --> 00:00:42,125
Five steps.

10
00:00:42,125 --> 00:00:45,250
One: read the error, properly, from the bottom.

11
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:50,650
Two: inspect the objects you have, rather than relying on what you believe they contain.

12
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:54,775
Three: check the documentation for your version of the library.

13
00:00:54,775 --> 00:00:59,000
Four: ask for help well, whether from a person or an assistant.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,650
Five: verify what you are given, and this last step matters most when the code came from an AI assistant.

15
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:10,625
Notice that the first two cost nothing and solve a large fraction of problems.

16
00:01:10,625 --> 00:01:17,425
The temptation when something breaks is to change a line and run it again, and change another line and run it again.

17
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:20,675
That is guessing, and it is slower than it feels.

18
00:01:21,275 --> 00:01:26,675
Python tracebacks are printed in an unhelpful order, so here is how to read one.

19
00:01:26,675 --> 00:01:28,925
Start at the last line.

20
00:01:28,925 --> 00:01:33,000
That is the error type and the message: what actually went wrong.

21
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,175
Then look upwards for the arrow pointing at a line of your own code.

22
00:01:37,175 --> 00:01:43,150
The traceback includes frames from inside the library, which are usually not where your problem is.

23
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:47,825
Learn the common error types, because each one means something specific.

24
00:01:47,825 --> 00:01:55,650
A KeyError on a DataFrame means there is no column with that name, which is usually a typo or a different spelling than you remember.

25
00:01:55,650 --> 00:02:02,775
An AttributeError means the object does not have the thing you asked for, which usually means it is not the type you think it is.

26
00:02:02,775 --> 00:02:05,825
An IndexError means you have gone off the end.

27
00:02:05,825 --> 00:02:09,775
A TypeError means you combined two incompatible things.

28
00:02:09,775 --> 00:02:16,550
The thing to resist is reading the first line and panicking because it mentions a file deep inside pandas.

29
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:19,175
The last line is the message.

30
00:02:19,175 --> 00:02:21,000
Read it first.

31
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:26,400
Second step, and this is the one that separates fast debugging from slow.

32
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,500
When something is wrong, look at the object.

33
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,475
Do not remember the object; look at it.

34
00:02:32,475 --> 00:02:35,325
Five things answer most questions.

35
00:02:35,325 --> 00:02:38,625
type of x, to find out what it actually is.

36
00:02:38,625 --> 00:02:41,625
The shape, for an array or DataFrame.

37
00:02:41,625 --> 00:02:46,000
The columns, for a DataFrame, which tells you what the names really are.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,200
The length.

39
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,750
And simply printing the first few elements, or head.

40
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:56,875
The reason this works is that most bugs are a mismatch between what you believe you have and what you have.

41
00:02:56,875 --> 00:03:00,650
You believe it is a DataFrame; it is a Series.

42
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:05,850
You believe the column is called g c; it is g c underscore content.

43
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:10,525
You believe you have a thousand rows; a filter removed most of them.

44
00:03:10,525 --> 00:03:13,375
Five seconds of looking resolves it.

45
00:03:13,375 --> 00:03:19,050
Minutes of reasoning about what it should be does not, because you are reasoning from the wrong premise.

46
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:22,375
Third step, and a specific trap.

47
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:24,350
Libraries change.

48
00:03:24,350 --> 00:03:27,900
Functions are renamed, deprecated, removed.

49
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:32,225
The example on the slide: Biopython's GC function.

50
00:03:32,225 --> 00:03:35,575
Hundreds of tutorials on the web still use it.

51
00:03:35,575 --> 00:03:45,325
It no longer exists; it is called gc underscore fraction now, and it returns a fraction rather than a percentage, so even the meaning of the number changed.

52
00:03:45,325 --> 00:03:47,800
Where will you meet the old name?

53
00:03:47,800 --> 00:04:01,325
In old tutorials, in old StackOverflow answers, in blog posts, and, importantly, from AI assistants, which were trained on all of that text and will cheerfully give you code using a function that was removed three years ago.

54
00:04:01,325 --> 00:04:06,600
So the authority is the documentation for the version you actually have installed.

55
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:10,250
Not the tutorial, not the assistant, not your memory.

56
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:13,275
And you can check your version in one line.

57
00:04:13,275 --> 00:04:22,600
This is a small thing that costs a surprising amount of time, because the error message, name is not defined, does not tell you that the function used to exist.

58
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:27,425
Fourth and fifth steps, together, since you will all use these tools.

59
00:04:27,425 --> 00:04:29,400
How to ask well.

60
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:32,700
Give the assistant what you would give a human helper.

61
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,850
The full error message, not your paraphrase.

62
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:38,975
A sample of the data, df dot head.

63
00:04:38,975 --> 00:04:41,450
The versions of the libraries.

64
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:45,150
What you expected to happen, and what you already tried.

65
00:04:45,150 --> 00:04:48,250
And never ask only "fix my code".

66
00:04:48,250 --> 00:04:50,900
Say what the code is supposed to do.

67
00:04:50,900 --> 00:05:01,300
An assistant that does not know your intent will make the error disappear, which is not the same as making the code correct, and those two diverge exactly in the cases that matter.

68
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:04,175
Then verify, which is not optional.

69
00:05:04,175 --> 00:05:07,775
Run it on a tiny example where you know the answer.

70
00:05:07,775 --> 00:05:11,450
Check the shapes and the ranges of whatever comes out.

71
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:17,250
Look up any function you have not seen before, in the documentation, because it may not exist.

72
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:24,575
And the specific danger, which is this session's theme again: an assistant is very good at producing code that runs.

73
00:05:24,575 --> 00:05:27,200
Kind one mistakes get fixed.

74
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:35,200
Kinds two and three do not, and a confident explanation attached to a wrong analysis is harder to doubt than your own code would have been.

75
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:41,075
A small habit that pays for itself: write your assumptions down as assertions.

76
00:05:41,075 --> 00:05:45,050
You believe your DataFrame has three thousand and one rows.

77
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:46,550
Assert it.

78
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:50,250
You believe every p-value is between nought and one.

79
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:51,750
Assert it.

80
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:56,650
You believe no protein in your table is longer than a thousand residues.

81
00:05:56,650 --> 00:05:58,150
Assert it.

82
00:05:58,150 --> 00:06:05,525
An assert is one line, and it turns a belief into a check that stops the notebook, loudly, at the moment it stops being true.

83
00:06:05,525 --> 00:06:09,750
The value is not in the first run, where you already know it holds.

84
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:16,300
It is six weeks later, when you have changed the filtering upstream and quietly broken something downstream.

85
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:20,925
Without the assertion, that breakage flows into your results silently.

86
00:06:20,925 --> 00:06:24,100
With it, you get an error at the exact line.

87
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:31,800
This is how you convert kind two and kind three mistakes into kind one mistakes, which is the best trade available to you.

88
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,550
A trap that is specific to notebooks and that catches everyone at least once.

89
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:42,000
A notebook's memory and the notebook you can see are two different things.

90
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,650
You can run cells out of order.

91
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:47,025
You can run a cell twice.

92
00:06:47,025 --> 00:06:49,925
You can edit a cell after running it.

93
00:06:49,925 --> 00:06:53,775
You can delete a cell whose variables are still defined.

94
00:06:53,775 --> 00:06:59,200
In every one of those cases, the state in memory does not match the code on the page.

95
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:08,050
The demonstration in the notebook: a cell that adds one to a counter gives a different answer every time you run it, although its code never changes.

96
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:13,500
So the result you are looking at may not be the result that your code, as written, would produce.

97
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:17,300
It may depend on something you deleted twenty minutes ago.

98
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:25,250
The discipline: before you trust a result, and always before you share a notebook, restart the kernel and run all cells from the top.

99
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:28,650
If it does not survive that, it was not a result.

100
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:35,100
And version control, briefly, because it is the other half of being able to trust your own work.

101
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:38,400
Git keeps a history of snapshots of your files.

102
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:41,075
The practical advice is short.

103
00:07:41,075 --> 00:07:46,175
Commit small, working steps rather than one enormous change at the end of the week.

104
00:07:46,175 --> 00:07:49,175
Write messages that say why, not what.

105
00:07:49,175 --> 00:07:53,925
The diff already shows what changed; what it cannot show is the reason.

106
00:07:53,925 --> 00:07:58,325
And never commit large data, passwords or API keys.

107
00:07:58,325 --> 00:08:00,550
Put them in gitignore.

108
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:08,350
A key committed once is in the history for ever, even if you delete the file afterwards, and this happens distressingly often.

109
00:08:08,350 --> 00:08:11,200
One practical point for this course.

110
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:18,825
Cloning the course repository on your laptop gives you all the notebooks; open them in VS Code and run them on a Colab machine.

111
00:08:18,825 --> 00:08:28,575
And keep the two places straight in your head: git works on the files on your laptop, while anything a notebook writes while it runs is on the Colab machine and disappears with it.

112
00:08:29,175 --> 00:08:34,400
Now something for you to do, and I recommend trying it before reading the solution.

113
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:48,425
The six globins again, done properly this time: fetch them, tabulate them, and produce the untranslated-region figure from the live coding, with the protein lengths computed from the CDS features rather than from the mRNA length.

114
00:08:48,425 --> 00:08:54,175
That is a complete small analysis: get data, structure it, compute, plot, interpret.

115
00:08:54,175 --> 00:08:59,550
The accession numbers are on the slide, and the worked solution is in the course notebook.

116
00:08:59,550 --> 00:09:08,900
The reason to try before reading is that the solution will look obvious when you see it, and obvious-when-seen is a very poor measure of whether you could have written it.

117
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:13,200
And the lab, which has three stages of decreasing help.

118
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:17,800
Fill in: the code is written, with pieces missing, marked with a to-do.

119
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:20,000
You supply the pieces.

120
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,375
Adapt: working code for a related problem, which you change to do what is asked.

121
00:09:25,375 --> 00:09:28,175
Solve: a question, and you write it.

122
00:09:28,175 --> 00:09:31,950
One warning about the adapt stage, which is deliberate.

123
00:09:31,950 --> 00:09:37,925
Of the three bugs you have to find there, one runs without any error at all and gives a wrong answer.

124
00:09:37,925 --> 00:09:44,850
That is a kind two mistake, planted on purpose, and if you only check whether the code runs you will not find it.

125
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:53,125
There are also three optional extras if you have time: fetching leptin with Entrez, some seaborn plotting, and a Mann-Whitney comparison.

126
00:09:53,725 --> 00:09:59,825
One last slide before the summary, and it is the question I would like you to carry out of this session.

127
00:09:59,825 --> 00:10:03,600
AI assistants no longer only suggest snippets.

128
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:11,300
Agents now take a question, retrieve tools and data, plan an analysis, write the code, run it, read the errors and try again.

129
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:24,000
Biomni, from Stanford in twenty twenty-five, is one example: a hundred and fifty biomedical tools, a hundred and five software packages, fifty-nine databases, driven from a question in English.

130
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:30,575
Now apply this session's taxonomy to that, because it tells you exactly what changes and what does not.

131
00:10:30,575 --> 00:10:33,875
Kind one mistakes, Python errors, stop the run.

132
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:38,975
The agent sees them and fixes them, often better and faster than you would.

133
00:10:38,975 --> 00:10:42,425
Kind two, a wrong analysis, runs silently.

134
00:10:42,425 --> 00:10:45,900
The axes are swapped, and nothing is reported.

135
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:49,900
Kind three, biologically nonsensical, runs silently.

136
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:55,850
The six-hundred-and-fifty-four-residue globin appears in the table, and nothing is reported.

137
00:10:55,850 --> 00:11:02,400
So automation removes the easy kind of mistake and leaves both hard kinds exactly where they were.

138
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:10,750
And the habits from this session, plot first, check a case you know, assert your assumptions, are precisely the checks that such a result still needs.

139
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:16,575
Then the question on the slide: who is responsible for a result that no human ever looked at?

140
00:11:16,575 --> 00:11:19,100
I will not answer that for you.

141
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:28,150
I will note that the course rules are unambiguous: AI-written labs and reports are not allowed, and the reason is that the checking is the skill being taught.

142
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:32,325
The summary, back to the four ideas from the beginning.

143
00:11:32,325 --> 00:11:40,450
Represent: choose the object that fits your data, because the object decides which operations you get and which mistakes are possible.

144
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:43,225
Explore: look before you compute.

145
00:11:43,225 --> 00:11:45,375
Describe and plot.

146
00:11:45,375 --> 00:11:51,050
The pre-filtered p-values and the gap in the histogram were the same fact, found two ways.

147
00:11:51,050 --> 00:11:58,225
Use what exists: standard questions have standard functions, written by people who thought about the edge cases.

148
00:11:58,225 --> 00:12:01,325
Get unstuck: a routine, not guessing.

149
00:12:01,325 --> 00:12:07,400
Read the error from the bottom, inspect the object, check the documentation, ask well, verify.

150
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:15,225
And the one rule, which is the sentence I asked you to leave with: code running without an error does not mean the analysis is correct.

151
00:12:15,225 --> 00:12:19,650
Every session after this one builds models that produce numbers.

152
00:12:19,650 --> 00:12:23,025
This session is about whether you should believe them.

153
00:12:23,625 --> 00:12:25,750
That is Session two.

154
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:38,225
Session three is biological databases: what is actually in UniProt, GenBank, the PDB and the rest, how they relate to each other, and how to get things out of them in a way that will still work next year.

155
00:12:38,225 --> 00:12:48,025
The lab follows one gene, HBB, from its sequence to its structure, its interactions and its literature, which is the dossier a real project starts with.

156
00:12:48,025 --> 00:12:57,325
The Session two page has all of this written out, including the mini challenge and the chatbot rules, and the course notebook is linked in the box under its title.

157
00:12:57,325 --> 00:12:58,950
See you there.

